{
"dur": 45.054,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 5.358,
"text": "Every January, Larry Fink writes a public letter to the bosses of the companies BlackRock invests in.",
"end": 5.633
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 5.633,
"vo_start": 5.908,
"vo_end": 12.505,
"text": "In 2020, the letter said that climate risk is investment risk, and that BlackRock would put sustainability at the centre of how it invests.",
"end": 12.78
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 12.78,
"vo_start": 13.055,
"vo_end": 16.524,
"text": "When the biggest owner in the market says that, every boardroom listens.",
"end": 16.799
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 16.799,
"vo_start": 17.074,
"vo_end": 26.994,
"text": "Then the backlash came from the other side. Republican-led states called it political, and some pulled money out. Florida alone moved about two billion dollars away in 2022.",
"end": 27.269
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 27.269,
"vo_start": 27.544,
"vo_end": 36.794,
"text": "BlackRock changed its tone. It let more of its fund investors cast their own votes, and in 2025 it left the climate alliance Net Zero Asset Managers.",
"end": 37.069
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 37.069,
"vo_start": 37.344,
"vo_end": 44.104,
"text": "Attacked from the left for not doing enough, and from the right for doing too much. That's what happens when you own a slice of everyone.",
"end": 45.054
}
],
